  "tarball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe/tarball/sp11-ubuntu-concept-26.04-v23-20260905",
  "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe/zipball/sp11-ubuntu-concept-26.04-v23-20260905",
  "body": "# Ubuntu Concept live ISO for Surface Pro 11\r\n\r\nExperimental Ubuntu Concept 26.04 ARM64 live media with the custom **v23 kernel**, Wi-Fi board data prepared before boot, the latest development Lexr, the source-bearing IPTSD bundle, and `LEXR_GETTING_STARTED.txt` on the desktop. This is an unofficial remaster for community testing.\r\n\r\nThe tested hardware target is **Surface Pro 11 X1E/OLED**. X1P/LCD and other Snapdragon devices remain unqualified. Disable Secure Boot for the unsigned custom kernel.\r\n\r\n## What has been tested\r\n\r\nThe preceding image built from Lexr `384f2c0` booted the X1E/OLED live desktop, connected to Wi-Fi immediately, and opened the installer welcome screen. The tester installed the included `sp11-iptsd-v2` bundle with the on-USB Lexr and confirmed pen and touchscreen input. Its full USB write and read-back SHA-256 matched.\r\n\r\nThis rebuilt image carries the same boot fixes plus the later IPTSD cleanup correction and release-journal packaging fix. The IPTSD correction was separately tested in that running live session: installation exited successfully, its receipt recorded complete activation, and the service remained active. **This exact rebuilt ISO has passed structural and reconstructed-byte validation; it has not yet had a separate physical USB boot.**\r\n\r\nInstallation, installed-system boot, audio setup, pressure/palm rejection and suspend/resume remain separate tests. `doctor hardware wifi` can still report unavailable device-tree inspection and a newer-than-v19 compatibility warning even when the radio works. See [Lexr PR #47](https://github.com/ooaklee/lexr.sh/pull/47), [Ubuntu live boot #41](https://github.com/ooaklee/lexr.sh/issues/41), and [installation/peripheral qualification #16](https://github.com/ooaklee/lexr.sh/issues/16).\r\n\r\n## Exact inputs\r\n\r\n- Ubuntu source: `resolute-desktop-arm64+x1e-20260326.iso`, SHA-256 `d0cbef7b48f5806093c2f4d8ea6d372249e86ace0051217c76ce92d60274078d`.\r\n- Kernel: [`7.2.0-jg-0sp11v23-qcom-x1e`](https://github.com/ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe/releases/tag/sp11-qcom-x1e-7.2.0-jg-0sp11v23).\r\n- Builder and embedded Lexr: `v0.3.0-10-g872ecf9`, commit [`872ecf90df03dafd3f56dfd326fe79765bb439ed`](https://github.com/ooaklee/lexr.sh/commit/872ecf90df03dafd3f56dfd326fe79765bb439ed).\r\n- IPTSD: [`sp11-iptsd-v2`](https://github.com/ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe/releases/tag/sp11-iptsd-v2), with corresponding source and licences in the companion.\r\n- OE tag anchor: `aba8c0b65ea73422c8e15868606bb934780a066e`, the support commit used by the v23 kernel release. The separate Lexr commit above identifies the image builder.\r\n- Output ISO: `sp11-ubuntu-concept-26.04-v23-20260905.iso`; **4629463040 bytes**, SHA-256 **`408ea746c472666aa86c71430de005aef916376205e65c65c5060eece4ee9607`**.\r\n\r\n## Download, verify and write with Lexr\r\n\r\nUse Linux or macOS, Go 1.26 or newer, Git, `zstd`, and Docker with Linux ARM64 support. GitHub CLI is used for the download below; downloading every listed release asset manually into the same fresh directory also works. Allow space for the compressed parts, reconstructed ISO and validation workspace.\r\n\r\nUse the latest Lexr version for the new image/release contract; you should use a lexr version of at least `v0.3.0`. From a fresh working directory:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ooaklee/lexr.sh/refs/heads/main/install.sh | sh -s\r\n\r\nLEXR=\"$(command -v lexr)\"\r\n\"$LEXR\" version\r\n\r\n\r\ngh release download sp11-ubuntu-concept-26.04-v23-20260905 \\\r\n  --repo ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe --dir image-assets\r\n(cd image-assets && shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS && \"$LEXR\" image release validate .)\r\n\r\nset -o pipefail\r\nISO=sp11-ubuntu-concept-26.04-v23-20260905.iso\r\ncat \"image-assets/$ISO\".zst.part-* | zstd --decompress --stdout > \"$ISO\"\r\nprintf '%s  %s\\n' '408ea746c472666aa86c71430de005aef916376205e65c65c5060eece4ee9607' \"$ISO\" | shasum -a 256 -c -\r\n\"$LEXR\" image validate \"$ISO\"\r\n\r\n\"$LEXR\" image devices\r\nDEVICE=\"<reviewed-whole-USB-device>\"\r\n\"$LEXR\" image write \"$ISO\" --device \"$DEVICE\" --dry-run\r\nsudo \"$LEXR\" image write \"$ISO\" --device \"$DEVICE\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nReplace `DEVICE` with the whole removable USB device identified by Lexr. Review the plan and enter Lexr's exact confirmation for that device and image. Lexr performs the write, complete read-back verification and ejection. Reinsert the USB before booting its default X1E/OLED entry.\r\n\r\n`SHA256SUMS` covers all published assets except itself. Keep the downloaded directory closed when running `image release validate`; reconstruct the ISO outside it as shown. The original journal is retained privately; only its path-free evidence is published. The generated `RELEASE-NOTES.md` contains the deterministic reconstruction contract.\r\n\r\n## In the live desktop\r\n\r\nOpen `LEXR_GETTING_STARTED.txt` on the desktop. Wi-Fi board data is already prepared from Ubuntu's distribution firmware. Install the included IPTSD userspace for pen and touchscreen input:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nCOMPANION_ROOT=/cdrom/sp11/companion\r\nmkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/bin\"\r\nTOOL=\"$HOME/.local/bin/lexr\"\r\ninstall -m 0755 \"$COMPANION_ROOT/bin/linux-arm64/lexr\" \"$TOOL\"\r\nIPTSD_ROOT=\"$COMPANION_ROOT/userspace/iptsd-v1/sp11-iptsd-v2\"\r\n\"$TOOL\" userspace install iptsd --from \"$IPTSD_ROOT\" --dry-run\r\nsudo \"$TOOL\" userspace install iptsd --from \"$IPTSD_ROOT\" --yes\r\n\"$TOOL\" doctor userspace --feature iptsd\r\n```\r\n\r\nRepeat relevant userspace setup after installation: live-session changes are temporary. Audio needs the matching FullIO userspace and private same-device firmware; configure it from the installed system on internal storage, following the desktop guide. Restarting the audio DSP during a live USB session can disconnect its root disk. No private firmware, SSH keys, ngrok configuration or debugging helper is included.\r\n\r\nThe ISO embeds Lexr's corresponding source and notices and the IPTSD source-bearing release. The [v23 kernel release](https://github.com/ooaklee/linux-surface-pro-11-oe/releases/tag/sp11-qcom-x1e-7.2.0-jg-0sp11v23) provides the exact kernel corresponding source, licence evidence and checksums. Distribution packages and firmware retain their supplied licences and notices.\r\n",
